{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "diagnose-slow-query",
  "domain": "performance-and-diagnostics",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "corrects-a-wrong-assumption"],
  "correction": "Agents troubleshooting a slow Azure SQL Database query treat Query Store as an automatic, complete record of everything that ran, because it is enabled by default on every database, when its default AUTO capture mode applies internal thresholds that silently drop ad hoc and infrequently executed queries, so the exact query someone ran once and is asking about can be completely absent from every Query Store view even immediately after a manual flush, and checking the plan cache instead does not rescue the diagnosis, because a query can hold a cached compiled plan with a nonzero use count and still have zero matching rows in sys.dm_exec_query_stats.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer treats the absence of the statement from the built-in history as a property of what that history captures rather than as evidence about the query, and gives the reader a way to make this statement recordable or to reach the evidence another way.",
    "task": "One of our reports on Azure SQL Database took about forty seconds this morning when it normally takes two. It has already finished, so I cannot catch it while it is running. I went looking in the database's own built-in history of what ran and I cannot find the statement at all, not even straight after re-running it once by hand to see if that helped. Does that mean it was never slow, or that something is off about how I am looking? Walk me through how you would work out what happened and which numbers you would want in front of you.",
    "states": [
      "The default capture mode applies internal thresholds that drop ad hoc and infrequently executed statements.",
      "Duration columns in that history are in microseconds, so a one second execution reads as one million.",
      "A statement can hold a cached plan with a nonzero use count and still have no matching rows in the execution statistics view.",
      "A single average duration is not a verdict, because the spread across executions is what separates the possible causes."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "Everything that runs is recorded, so a statement missing from the history did not run slowly or did not run at all.",
      "Flushing the history to disk makes an ad hoc statement that was dropped appear in it.",
      "Looking in the plan cache instead reliably rescues the diagnosis when the statement is missing from the history.",
      "The duration columns in that history are in milliseconds."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "inspect", "execute"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "diagnose-resource-pressure",
    "diagnose-blocking-and-deadlocks",
    "read-execution-plan",
    "capture-with-extended-events"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a table with enough rows that a scan and a seek cost visibly different amounts",
      "the same query executed a handful of times with parameter values that make its row count vary widely",
      "a second, different plan produced for the same query id, for example by forcing a table scan once and an index seek once",
      "a query executed exactly once under the default AUTO query capture mode, never repeated",
      "a session left open with an uncommitted transaction while a second session runs the query under test"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "sys.database_query_store_options reports actual_state READ_WRITE on a newly created database with no configuration step taken",
      "a query executed only once under AUTO capture mode is correctly named as possibly absent from sys.query_store_query, rather than assumed present",
      "the coefficient of variation, stdev_duration divided by avg_duration from sys.query_store_runtime_stats, is used to rank instability rather than a single avg_duration value read alone",
      "a query id carrying more than one plan_id in sys.query_store_plan is read as a plan change over time, and the better plan is forced with sys.sp_query_store_force_plan rather than the worse plan simply being removed",
      "a session with a nonzero blocking_session_id in sys.dm_exec_requests is diagnosed as blocked and handed to diagnose-blocking-and-deadlocks instead of being treated as a plan or resource problem",
      "wait composition read from sys.query_store_wait_stats is used to decide whether the next step is diagnose-resource-pressure or diagnose-blocking-and-deadlocks, not guessed"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT actual_state_desc, CASE WHEN query_capture_mode_desc IN ('ALL', 'AUTO', 'NONE', 'CUSTOM') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS capture_mode_is_a_documented_value FROM sys.database_query_store_options;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              "READ_WRITE",
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The claim the first paragraph turns on: Query Store is already collecting on a database nobody configured, so enabling it is never step one. Measured 2026-08-29 on a database created moments earlier, and Microsoft Learn states Query Store is enabled by default for new Azure SQL Database databases and cannot be turned off there. The capture mode is asserted only as one of the four documented values rather than as AUTO, because two Learn pages disagree about the default for SQL Server builds and the skill tells the reader to read the mode rather than assume it. No probe asserts that AUTO drops a given query: the skill's own measurements show a cheap query absent after four executions while a costlier JOIN run 30 times was captured, so the threshold is not a fixed count and an absence assertion would be flaky. That claim stays prose."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SET NOCOUNT ON;\nALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET QUERY_STORE (QUERY_CAPTURE_MODE = ALL);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_qs_norm (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_qs_norm VALUES (1), (2);\nDECLARE @rc int, @tag nvarchar(40) = N'PROBEQS' + N'TAG', @obj nvarchar(40) = N'probe_qs' + N'_norm';\nSELECT @rc = COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_qs_norm WHERE id > 0 /*PROBEQSTAG*/;\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_flush_db;\nSELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.query_store_query_text WHERE query_sql_text LIKE N'%' + @tag + N'%') AS comment_matches, CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.query_store_query_text WHERE query_sql_text LIKE N'%COUNT(*)%' AND query_sql_text LIKE N'%' + @obj + N'%') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS found_by_bare_name, CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.query_store_query_text WHERE query_sql_text LIKE N'%COUNT(*)%' AND query_sql_text LIKE N'%[[]dbo].[[]probe_qs_norm]%') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS found_by_bracketed_name;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0,
              1,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The normalization trap, made executable, in the three columns that tell its halves apart. The comment never survives capture, so column one is 0. The statement IS captured and is found by the bare object name, so column two is 1. The bracketed form is NOT there, so column three is 0, and that is the correction this probe exists for: the brackets and the @N placeholders arrive together as one parameterization rewrite, marked by the (@1 tinyint) prefix Microsoft Learn documents, so they are absent from a statement the engine did not rewrite. Measured by the lane on 2026-09-03, when an earlier version of this probe searched only the bracketed form and returned 0 while the force plan probe found the same statement by its bare name in the same run: captured, and unbracketed. Both patterns also require COUNT(*), which the fixture INSERT does not have, so neither column can be satisfied by anything but the workload statement; the tag and the object name are rebuilt from two literals each so the searching statement cannot match itself. QUERY_CAPTURE_MODE is set to ALL first, because the default AUTO is what the skill says drops a query this cheap."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_qs_force (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, pad char(50) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_qs_force (id, pad) SELECT TOP (200) ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY (SELECT NULL)), 'x' FROM sys.all_objects;\nALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET QUERY_STORE (QUERY_CAPTURE_MODE = ALL);\nDECLARE @rc int, @qid bigint, @pid bigint;\nSELECT @rc = COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_qs_force WHERE id > 10;\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_flush_db;\nSELECT TOP (1) @qid = q.query_id, @pid = p.plan_id FROM sys.query_store_query q JOIN sys.query_store_query_text t ON q.query_text_id = t.query_text_id JOIN sys.query_store_plan p ON p.query_id = q.query_id WHERE t.query_sql_text LIKE '%COUNT(*)%probe%force%' AND t.query_sql_text NOT LIKE '%query_store%' ORDER BY q.query_id DESC;\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_force_plan @query_id = @qid, @plan_id = @pid;\nSELECT is_forced_plan, force_failure_count, last_force_failure_reason FROM sys.query_store_plan WHERE plan_id = @pid;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              0,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Step 4 tells the reader to force the better plan, and the Check it worked section tells them to read the result back rather than trust the EXEC. This runs both halves: sys.sp_query_store_force_plan with the two parameters Microsoft Learn documents, then the three columns that say whether the forcing took. is_forced_plan 1 with force_failure_count 0 and last_force_failure_reason 0 is the clean outcome; Learn lists 8712 NO_INDEX, 8698 NO_PLAN and 8690 HINT_CONFLICT among the nonzero reasons. Forcing requires ALTER on the database, which the administrative connection a probe runs on has. The workload query assigns its count to a variable so the batch emits one rowset; the recorded text still carries COUNT(*) and the table name the lookup matches on, with no brackets assumed. This probe passing on 2026-09-03 is what proves an assignment SELECT is captured at all, which is the half of the normalization probe that the bracketed search could not distinguish. The lookup excludes text mentioning query_store, because under QUERY_CAPTURE_MODE = ALL the lookup statement is itself captured and its own pattern literal would otherwise match it, which would force a plan on the wrong query and still return a passing row."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT ws.wait_category_desc, SUM(ws.total_query_wait_time_ms) AS total_wait_ms FROM sys.query_store_wait_stats ws WHERE ws.plan_id = -1 GROUP BY ws.wait_category_desc ORDER BY total_wait_ms DESC;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": []
        },
        "why": "Step 6 routes on wait category, so the view and both of the columns it names have to exist on the engine this skill runs against. sys.query_store_wait_stats is documented for SQL Server 2017 and later and for Azure SQL Database, and carries wait_category_desc alongside the numeric wait_category, which is why no mapping table is needed in the body. plan_id = -1 can match nothing, so an empty result is the whole claim: the statement parsed and every column resolved."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.dm_exec_requests WHERE session_id = @@SPID AND blocking_session_id = 0) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS own_request_is_unblocked;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Step 1 sends the reader to sys.dm_exec_requests before any Query Store read, and routes on blocking_session_id. This asserts the DMV and that column exist and that an unblocked request reads 0 there, which is the value the whole step-1 branch is written against. The probe's own session is by definition not blocked while it is executing."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "This query used to run in under a second and now it takes ten seconds, find out why",
      "Diagnose why this stored procedure is slow",
      "Use diagnose-slow-query on this report query"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "Sometimes this dashboard finishes in two seconds and sometimes it takes forty, same parameters",
      "This query got slow right after last night's deployment and nothing else changed",
      "Query Store shows nothing for the query I just ran, does that mean it was fast",
      "The nightly job used to take ten minutes and now it takes forty five, and it keeps creeping up"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "why is this slow",
      "this report takes forever now"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Why is CPU pinned at 100 percent on this database",
      "Two sessions look stuck waiting on each other, one of them is a deadlock victim",
      "Read this execution plan XML and tell me which operator is expensive",
      "Set up a session to capture the next occurrence of this automatically",
      "Rewrite this query so it does not use a cursor",
      "How do I create an index on this table"
    ]
  }
}
